SU(2) three-condition wall-payment exhaustive obstruction

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research analyze_su2_aim3_exhaustive
./analyze_su2_aim3_exhaustive 4 12 24

OUTPUT
SU2_AIM3_EXHAUSTIVE maximum_level=8 maximum_coefficient=12 maximum_length=24 threads=36 profiles=1252318 admissible_profiles=3736066 cap_checks=14956978 cap_failures=0 payment_checks=12477664 payment_failures=1108 stable_checks=12477664 stable_failures=17030
FIRST_CAP_FAILURE level=-1 factor=-1 label=-1 value=0 boundary=0 profile=[] reserve=[] caps=[]
FIRST_PAYMENT_FAILURE level=6 factor=1 label=0 value=-1 boundary=31 profile=[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,8,8,8,8] reserve=[0,0,0,8] caps=[8,8,8,8]
FIRST_STABLE_FAILURE level=6 factor=1 label=0 value=-1 boundary=31 profile=[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,8,8,8,8] reserve=[0,0,0,8] caps=[8,8,8,8]

METHOD
The analyzer recursively exhausts every positive integer profile of
length 2 through 24, with every coefficient at most 12, whose
successive choices satisfy both exact log concavity and

  (2t+3)m_t >= (2t+1)m_(t+1).

It interprets the end of every enumerated prefix as a terminal zero.
For every half-level 2<=K<=4, it retains exactly those profiles whose
first-wall reserve u_1 is coefficientwise nonnegative.  It then
checks the three-image cap, the exact T_q payment for every reduced
factor and active row, and the larger stable-weight payment.  All
arithmetic is signed 64-bit integer arithmetic; the stated coefficient
and length bounds keep every intermediate far below the type limit.

The first exact payment failure is at K=3, q=1, r=0:

  m=(1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,8,8,8,8),
  u_1=(0,0,0,8),
  (T_1)_(0,*)=(0,-2,-3,-4),
  rho=m_6+m_7+m_8+m_9=31.

Thus rho-4u_(1,3)=31-32=-1.  Its ratios

  2,3/2,4/3,5/4,6/5,7/6,8/7,1,1,1,1

are nonincreasing, so the profile is log concave.  The normalized-
dimension inequalities also hold exactly.  This single witness
disproves the proposed three-condition wall-payment lemma; the census
is only a bounded audit of the implementation.

The witness is the K=3 member of the exact family

  L=2K+2,
  m_t=min(t+1,L) for 0<=t<=L+K,
  m_t=0 afterwards,

which has u_1=(0,...,0,L) and q=1,r=0 payment margin
(4L-1)-4L=-1 for every K>=3.  Hence the obstruction is uniform and
does not depend on the bounded enumeration.

The adaptive thread count reflects current machine load and can
differ on replay; enumeration and exact counts are scheduling
independent.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror -pthread \
  analyze_su2_aim3_exhaustive.cpp \
  -o analyze_su2_aim3_exhaustive

